data (numbers) to perform an addition

18
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:30.258
operation.

19
00:01:30.258 --> 00:01:36.711
NARRATOR: On screen, show a diagram with three
interconnected circles labeled Hardware,

20
00:01:36.711 --> 00:01:43.164
Software, and Data, with arrows indicating the
flow of information between them.

21
00:01:43.164 --> 00:01:50.054
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

22
00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:52.351
evidence would distinguish them.

23
00:01:52.351 --> 00:01:58.633
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

24
00:01:58.754 --> 00:02:04.404
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Problem Decomposition and

25
00:02:04.404 --> 00:02:08.000
Sequential Steps operates in a concrete
sequence.

26
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.188
NARRATOR: Students will develop step-by- step
written solutions to basic problems and

27
00:02:14.188 --> 00:02:17.797
implement their solutions using a programming
language.

28
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:24.290
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will write a pseudocode sequence for a 'find

29
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:30.782
