list, and prints the maximum value.

40
00:03:23.362 --> 00:03:30.027
NARRATOR: On screen, display a code editor
window with syntax highlighting, showing

41
00:03:30.027 --> 00:03:34.471
variable declarations, a for-loop, and a print
statement.

42
00:03:34.471 --> 00:03:41.196
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

43
00:03:41.196 --> 00:03:42.877
move the goalposts.

44
00:03:42.877 --> 00:03:49.549
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

45
00:03:49.658 --> 00:03:57.016
NARRATOR: Apply Data Types and Variables to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

46
00:03:57.016 --> 00:03:57.936
isolated definition.

47
00:03:57.936 --> 00:04:02.639
NARRATOR: Names are bound to objects within
namespaces and scopes.

48
00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:09.286
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will compare integer, float, and string

49
00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:14.826
variables, explaining how each type affects
data storage and operations.

50
00:04:14.826 --> 00:04:23.917
NARRATOR: On screen, show a table comparing
variable types (int, float, str), their memory

51
00:04:23.917 --> 00:04:26.514
representation, and example assignments.

52
00:04:26.514 --> 00:04:33.295
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

53
00:04:33.295 --> 00:04:34.748
at each step.

54
00:04:34.748 --> 00:04:39.920
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

55
00:04:40.034 --> 00:04:46.539
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Control
Flow and Conditionals, including what improves

56
00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:49.249
and what becomes more difficult.

57
00:04:49.249 --> 00:04:56.898
NARRATOR: The Python language reference
specifies lexical analysis, data model

58
00:04:56.898 --> 00:05:03.782
behavior, execution, expressions, statements,
functions, classes, exceptions, and module

59
00:05:03.782 --> 00:05:05.312
import semantics.

60
00:05:05.312 --> 00:05:11.697
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will implement an if-else statement to check

61
00:05:11.697 --> 00:05:19.064
if a user input is positive, negative, or
zero, and handle invalid input with a

62
00:05:19.064 --> 00:05:20.046
try-except block.

63
00:05:20.046 --> 00:05:27.277
NARRATOR: On screen, display a flowchart
showing the decision path for an if-else

64
00:05:27.277 --> 00:05:31.171
condition, with arrows branching to true/false
outcomes.

65
00:05:31.171 --> 00:05:37.719
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

66
00:05:37.719 --> 00:05:38.187
acceptable.

67
00:05:38.187 --> 00:05:45.608
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

68
00:05:45.722 --> 00:05:52.351
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Functions and Abstraction with an edge case

69
00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:56.218
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

70
00:05:56.218 --> 00:06:03.571
NARRATOR: Debugging separates syntax, runtime,
and logical failures, while decomposition and

71
00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:08.250
abstraction reduce the number of interacting
concerns.

72
00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:13.589
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will define a function that calculates the

73
00:06:13.589 --> 00:06:19.750
